<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Gender.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.3. RQ3: Cross-Model Comparisons of Systematic Bias ‣ 4. Results ‣ Race and Gender in LLM-Generated Personas: A Large-Scale Audit of 41 Occupations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows a clear split. Three providers—ChatGPT, Mistral, and DeepSeek—<em id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">lift women on average</em> (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+10.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>10.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+10.7</annotation></semantics></math> pp, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+6.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>6.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+6.9</annotation></semantics></math> pp, and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+9.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>9.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+9.1</annotation></semantics></math> pp, respectively), while <em id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Gemini</em> is the only model that <em id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">lowers women</em> around the median point (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-7.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-7.6</annotation></semantics></math> pp). Across all four, slopes are small, indicating little additional steepening or flattening of gender segregation by occupation. In practice, readers see two patterns: a general “lift” for women in three models with minimal stereotyping, and an “offset down” in Gemini, also with minimal stereotyping.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.21011v1/rq3_race.png" id="S4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/375;" aria-describedby="acmlabel8" width="476" height="375" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8. </span>Systematic racial representation by model. Each panel plots BLS percentages of racial groups against LLM outputs, with parity shown as the diagonal line. All models display clear bias patterns: Black workers are consistently underrepresented, Hispanic workers are overrepresented, and Asian and White workers show stereotype exaggeration. Gemini is slightly closer to parity than GPT-4, DeepSeek, or Mistral, but still departs from BLS distributions.<span id="acmlabel8" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">Grid of scatterplots comparing BLS share (x-axis) to LLM share (y-axis) for Asian, Black, Hispanic, and White workers across GPT-4, DeepSeek, Mistral, and Gemini. Every model’s curves deviate from the parity diagonal—below for Black, above for Hispanic, and steep for Asian and White groups—showing bias throughout, with Gemini only modestly less skewed than the others.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5. </span>Systematic Racial Representation (By Model). Logistic regression estimates of systematic bias for racial representation, disaggregated by model. Notation as in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2. RQ2: Systematic Deviations and Stereotype Exaggeration ‣ 4. Results ‣ Race and Gender in LLM-Generated Personas: A Large-Scale Audit of 41 Occupations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</figcaption>
